From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 18:41:01 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: let Metal fetch vertex attributes where Metal
 can express them

kk_lower_vs_vbo bakes the vertex-input state into the vertex shader: every
attribute's format, its offset inside its buffer, the buffer's stride and its
step function become shader code that reads the buffers as UBOs.  That makes
the shader's MSL a function of state owned by the vertex-input interface
library, so a pipeline library holding the vertex shader cannot compile it
until the link, which is the other half of why
dEQP-VK.pipeline.pipeline_library.graphics_library.misc.other.compare_link_times
warns.

Metal describes the same fetch with an MTLVertexDescriptor on the
render-pipeline descriptor and hands the result to the vertex function through
[[stage_in]].  It has 54 vertex formats, per-attribute offsets, per-buffer
strides and a per-instance step function, so for most pipelines the descriptor
says everything kk_lower_vs_vbo used to compile in, and the vertex shader stops
depending on the vertex-input state entirely.  kk_vertex_input_is_metal_expressible
is the predicate; what Metal has no form of keeps the lowered path - BGRA
component order, USCALED and SSCALED, 64-bit and packed formats, and the
robustBufferAccess and robustnessBufferAccess2 fetch semantics, which Metal's
descriptor cannot promise.  A vertex stage poly turns into a compute dispatch
for tessellation or geometry keeps it too: it is not a Metal vertex function,
so it has neither a [[stage_in]] to fetch through nor a render-pipeline
descriptor to carry the vertex descriptor.

Two things had to follow the attributes out of the shader.  The vertex buffers
are now bound as addresses in the argument table rather than read as UBOs, at
KK_VERTEX_BUFFER_BINDING_BASE onwards, which is why the command buffer's
argument table now asks for every buffer bind Metal allows it.  And modify_nir_info used to clear inputs_read for
every vertex shader, on the assumption that attribute fetch always became
argument-buffer reads; a shader that lets Metal fetch keeps its attributes,
because they are exactly what the [[stage_in]] parameter and the vertex
descriptor are built from.  Clearing them left the MSL backend emitting
in.attribute_N against a parameter it had not declared.

The vertex descriptor is filled at each attribute's Vulkan location, because
that is the number the MSL backend names it by - both the VertexIn member and
its [[attribute(n)]] index come from the location.  Compacting the attributes
the way the shader-lowered path does, which indexes its own descriptor array,
instead left Metal matching a declared attribute against another one's format,
or against nothing: "Vertex attribute attribute_5(5) is missing from the vertex
descriptor".

Metal numbers both ends of that descriptor more narrowly than Vulkan numbers
its vertex input, so the predicate has to stop where Metal does.  An argument
table holds at most KK_MAX_ARGUMENT_TABLE_BUFFERS buffer binds - Metal 4's own
limit, which MTL4ArgumentTableDescriptor and MTLVertexAttributeDescriptor both
enforce - and three of those carry the root descriptor table, the sampler table
and the per-draw data, so Metal's fetch can reach KK_MAX_NATIVE_VBUFS bindings.
MSL numbers an [[attribute(n)]] from zero to thirty, so it can name
KK_MAX_NATIVE_VERTEX_ATTRIBS locations.  Vulkan advertises 32 of each.  The
lowered fetch has neither ceiling, because it reads its buffers through the
root descriptor table and indexes its own attribute array, so a binding or a
location past Metal's end keeps that path, and maxVertexInputBindings and
maxVertexInputAttributes stand as advertised.  Sizing the table at
KK_VERTEX_BUFFER_BINDING_BASE + KK_MAX_VBUFS instead asked for 35 binds, which
runtime Metal tolerated and MTLDebugDevice rejected outright - "max buffer bind
count must not be more than 31" - while a draw from the last Vulkan binding
aborted the process with "buffer index (34) must be < 31" with no validation
layer at all.

A zero attribute divisor keeps the lowered path.  Vulkan reads the element at
firstInstance there, not the first element of the buffer, and Metal's constant
step function always reads the first; firstInstance cannot be folded into the
bound address either, because an indirect draw keeps it in GPU memory.

The new kosmickrisp_metal_vertex_fetch test draws one fully covered target
from two bindings at once: an interleaved per-vertex buffer holding float32
positions, an 8-bit normalized colour and a 16-bit normalized coordinate at
three different offsets, and a separate per-instance buffer holding a float32
scale, with two instances covering different halves so an attribute that did
not advance per instance would show as two identical halves.  Every channel is
compared against the value the host decoded from the same bytes.  A second
draw uses a single attribute at location 5 and nothing else, the shape dEQP's
vertex_input single_attribute cases use, so a descriptor filled at a compacted
index rather than at the location fails it exactly as Metal reports it.  A
third draws two instances through a binding whose divisor is zero, which the
predicate now sends down the lowered path; it is kept because the Metal path
reached it before, and aborted the process inside MTLVertexDescriptor
validation.  A fourth and a fifth draw from the last binding and the last
location Vulkan advertises, both past Metal's ceilings and so on the lowered
path too, and read the same value back.  Four more link the same vertex stage
against the
generated fragment stages KosmicKrisp precompiles for point and line
rasterization - point and line topologies, and the point and line polygon
modes - because those are built separately and have to agree with a vertex
function that takes its attributes through [[stage_in]].  All pass on both
paths - MESA_KK_DEBUG=no_metal_vertex_fetch forces the lowered one, so
a suspected fixed-function fetch defect can be bisected without a rebuild.

A one-off benchmark measured the two paths against each other.  It drew
degenerate triangles into a tiny attachment, so the pass fetched and
transformed every vertex and shaded no fragment at all, over vertex buffers
small enough to stay resident - a working set larger than the caches makes
both paths read the same bytes at the same rate and hides what the fetch
itself costs.  Best of eight GPU-timed passes, six
alternating runs of each path on an M1 Ultra: four attributes interleaved
440.6 us against 453.0 us, the same four in separate buffers 435.9 us
against 453.5 us, four per-vertex plus one per-instance 439.9 us against
450.8 us, and eight attributes interleaved 436.6 us against 467.9 us.
Metal's fetch wins every case, by 2.5% to 4% at four attributes and 7.2%
at eight, which is the shape to expect when the cost removed is the
per-attribute address arithmetic and typed load the lowered path emits
into the shader.  Per vertex that is 0.278 ns against 0.298 ns at eight
attributes, and 6.82 us against 7.31 us per draw.  So Metal's fetch is
the default for monolithic pipelines too, not only where it enables a
fast link.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
